WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.346
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.995 --> 00:00:07.870
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to this week's episode

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.840
of "VM End to End," where
we have a VM skeptic

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.630
and a VM enthusiast come
together and hash out

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.820
all things Cloud VMs.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.988
Brian, thanks for being here.

00:00:17.988 --> 00:00:19.030
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.680
I think today is
networking, right?

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.240
CARTER MORGAN: It is
networking, because last time,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.190
you left me off feeling
really good about VMs.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.600
And I'm like, this can't stand.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.100
We've got to go deeper
and figure it out.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.590
So could you tell me, from
a big-picture overview, what

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.040
is networking like once
you're using Google's

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.948
infrastructure and all that?

00:00:37.948 --> 00:00:38.740
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.460
And honestly, I think this
is one of the main reasons

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.310
to consider running a
virtual machine in Google

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:49.450
Cloud, because the traffic
back and forth to the machine

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.090
runs mostly across the
network that Google built

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.730
to serve traffic for
things like Gmail,

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:58.210
and YouTube, and the like.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.220
So I think that's
the big reason.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.810
CARTER MORGAN: When you say
the network Google built,

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.060
that's a very precise
set of terminology.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.150
It sounds like someone actually
picked up tools and built

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.130
hardware for this.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.190
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, and
ran undersea fiber cable,

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.180
and built a whole
bunch of things.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.320
So we've got cables we've
laid and leased space

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.340
on other cables, all
to reduce the latency,

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.520
because the faster
your information

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.152
gets to people, the more--

00:01:30.152 --> 00:01:32.110
we've just seen research
that people bounce off

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.490
of pages that are slow.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.780
So you want to be fast--

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.920
as fast as you can be.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:36.670
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.400
So then what's this like for
people that aren't on Google?

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.970
Say, what is the network like
taking Google Cloud out of it?

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.890
What's it like for
a piece of traffic?

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.338
What happens?

00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:47.130
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.980
So if you've got
a machine that's

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.600
somewhere in a data center, or
actually even on a home network

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.030
somewhere, and somebody
else connects to it,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.862
it'll jump across a bunch of
hops and different routers

00:02:00.862 --> 00:02:02.070
and machines on the internet.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.900
And the packet makes its way
there and then makes its way

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:05.400
back.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.710
And we've kind of forgotten what
an amazing piece of technology

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.360
this is, the internet itself.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.833
It can route around problems
and get the traffic there.

00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:17.250
And it may take a
different route,

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.920
depending on how busy
things are and other stuff.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.200
But it does mean
that there is kind

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.473
of an unpredictability in
the route and the timing

00:02:24.473 --> 00:02:25.140
that that takes.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.730
So there's a certain amount of
variation that happens there.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:35.010
So one of the things we do is
built out dedicated networking.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.520
And the traffic will come
out of a Google machine,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.700
go across Google's network, and
then pop out somewhere close

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.955
to the end user--

00:02:42.955 --> 00:02:44.580
still on the public
internet, but fewer

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.830
hops between them and
the actual service.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.710
And I think that's the
key benefit, I guess,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.280
both for Google's services
and for yours that run here.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.680
And so I think at a high
level that it comes down

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.500
to consistency,
predictability of that.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.740
And then there's
another fun thing

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.780
that we'll dig into
here of just some

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.080
of these networking pieces
we offer as a service,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.550
rather than a particular piece
of hardware that's doing it.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.092
CARTER MORGAN: I've
heard of software

00:03:14.092 --> 00:03:15.990
as a service and
infrastructure as a service.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.030
I've never heard of
networking as a service.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.308
What does this mean, Brian?

00:03:19.308 --> 00:03:20.100
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.690
So the industry term for it is
software-defined networking.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.440
And the networks
that your VMs see

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.590
are an entirely
software-defined thing

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.680
and running on top of
the networking fabric

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.120
that runs the data center.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.550
So we've got this
extremely high-speed,

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.920
well-interconnected
physical network.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.410
And we run a software-defined
network on top of that.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.070
And that allows things like
firewalls and load balancers

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:50.265
to be provided, like again,
this slice of a data center,

00:03:50.265 --> 00:03:53.370
instead of a slice of
a particular computer.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.820
The data center is the
firewall and the data center

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.500
is the load balancer.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.893
CARTER MORGAN: A firewall is a
box that you plug in between--

00:04:02.893 --> 00:04:06.630
wait, so what do you mean the
firewall is a data center?

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:07.870
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.540
So instead of a box that you
plugin one side of the network

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.280
and the other side of
the network, the firewall

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.480
that you're using for
your VMs, and for deciding

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.620
what ports are open,
and that sort of thing,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:21.810
there are a set of rules.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:23.400
And then instead of
those rules going

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.090
to one box that's in
between, the implementation

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.450
of the software-defined network
itself enforces those rules.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.328
And that's distributed
across the whole data center.

00:04:33.328 --> 00:04:34.995
CARTER MORGAN: And
so instead of having,

00:04:34.995 --> 00:04:37.140
oh, my box can't
handle more, Google

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.370
can handle all of this for you.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.220
So you get firewall at scale--

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.613
firewalls at scale.

00:04:42.613 --> 00:04:43.530
BRIAN DORSEY: Exactly.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:44.910
And where that becomes
really important

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.190
is the load balancer at scale.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.020
And it seems really
counterintuitive.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.742
So instead of a particular
box that traffic hits

00:04:56.742 --> 00:04:58.450
and it spreads it
around to a whole bunch

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.580
of backend machines, right?

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.030
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.140
BRIAN DORSEY: Again,
you're setting rules.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:05.110
But still, it provides
this endpoint.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:09.310
It provides one IP address that
you see and routes the traffic

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.570
to all of them.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:13.955
But there's not just
one box doing it.

00:05:13.955 --> 00:05:15.830
CARTER MORGAN: I need
to know how this works,

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.300
because this is what Google--

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.158
like Gmail, YouTube
uses all this.

00:05:20.158 --> 00:05:21.075
BRIAN DORSEY: Exactly.

00:05:21.075 --> 00:05:21.615
CARTER MORGAN: How?

00:05:21.615 --> 00:05:23.850
BRIAN DORSEY: And in fact, we're
configuring those same load

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:24.390
balancers.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:29.730
And luckily for us, Google
published a paper about this.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.160
And the infrastructure
is called Maglev.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.010
So let's put a link to
that paper down below.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:40.157
But at a really high level,
it allows multiple computers--

00:05:40.157 --> 00:05:41.990
an arbitrary number of
them, so you can just

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.160
keep adding them--
to all implement

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.190
the same exact
load-balancing rules in sync

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.505
so that when you
change the rules or one

00:05:50.505 --> 00:05:52.380
of your backend machines
needs to go offline,

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.530
you bring a different one
up, all of the load balancing

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.110
cluster, essentially,
handles that for you.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.840
And there's distributed hashing
and a bunch of neat stuff

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.048
that makes that possible
that's listed in that paper.

00:06:03.048 --> 00:06:04.757
CARTER MORGAN: You're
giving me homework.

00:06:04.757 --> 00:06:06.240
I got to go read about this now.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.750
So this last episode, we touched
on networking a little bit

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.580
and you brought up
Managed Instance Groups,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:12.300
if I'm correct.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.320
BRIAN DORSEY: Yep.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.862
CARTER MORGAN: This
is the technology

00:06:14.862 --> 00:06:15.918
that makes that possible.

00:06:15.918 --> 00:06:16.710
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.120
So Managed Instance
Groups, I think,

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.960
are the next level
on top of that.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.170
So once you've got the
ability to route traffic

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:28.177
to a whole bunch of
different computers,

00:06:28.177 --> 00:06:29.760
how do you figure
out which computers,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.927
and whether they're working,
and that sort of thing?

00:06:31.927 --> 00:06:34.920
That's where Managed Instance
Groups come into play.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:35.670
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:40.380
And so something I keep
hearing just repeatedly,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.890
everyone says global.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:42.390
Global.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.000
Google has a global network.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.220
I don't necessarily
know what that means.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:50.778
Can you help clarify?

00:06:50.778 --> 00:06:51.570
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.290
And I guess I made the claim
that your Managed Instance

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:56.910
Group could have machines that
are spread around the globe,

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:57.410
right?

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:58.820
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.540
BRIAN DORSEY: So at some
level, it means exactly that.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:04.640
So our network--
we've put down fiber,

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.435
and we have data
centers that are

00:07:06.435 --> 00:07:08.060
in lots of different
places, and really

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.620
fast, as fast as possible.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.005
You still have
light speed there.

00:07:11.005 --> 00:07:12.380
CARTER MORGAN:
There are physics.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:13.100
BRIAN DORSEY: But as fast
as possible interconnect

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.940
between them.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.200
And I think the key
concepts to take away

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.340
as you're planning your
system is that we group

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:23.600
things in a hierarchy.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.080
So at the top,
you've got global,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.910
an IP address that
could be accessed

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.980
from anywhere in the world.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:33.780
And then regional,
which might be

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.990
something like Europe
or North America

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.840
or Asia, something like that.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.550
And then zonal.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.190
And conceptually, that's
kind of like a data center.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.850
It's at least a interconnected
set of hardware--

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:52.050
machines, networking,
power, redundancy--

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.830
that is all one unit.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.385
And the idea is that, if there
is a failure in one zone,

00:07:56.385 --> 00:07:57.760
it shouldn't affect
another zone.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.540
So you can choose to put
things in different zones

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.740
and trust that they're unlikely
to have the same issue that

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:05.730
affects each other.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:06.480
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.090
So then you get data redundancy
and backups, all of that,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.173
from zones.

00:08:10.173 --> 00:08:10.965
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:08:10.965 --> 00:08:14.410
So that's how you build
reliability into your system

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.170
so that you can keep
things separate.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:19.440
And the Managed Instance
Group can cross those.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.670
And it's really useful.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:24.510
And I think one key
thing to just keep

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.390
in the back of your head
is that communication

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:31.179
tends to be faster within
a zone and cheaper.

00:08:31.179 --> 00:08:32.929
CARTER MORGAN: Things
are closer together.

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.846
BRIAN DORSEY: And it
takes a little extra time

00:08:34.846 --> 00:08:37.588
and costs a little more
money to cross between zones.

00:08:37.588 --> 00:08:39.130
CARTER MORGAN: I
think at this point,

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:41.070
we've talked about
high-level benefits

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.840
of using Cloud's networking.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.510
I can't poke any holes
in the argument just now.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.960
It looks like it's very
useful for VM users.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.100
But I know that can't
be all of the benefits.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.500
So what I'm curious about, what
are the other core concepts?

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.500
We've talked high level.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.417
Are there any lower level
concepts that you're

00:09:00.417 --> 00:09:02.328
like, this is really cool?

00:09:02.328 --> 00:09:03.120
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.290
So we've already talked about
firewalls, load balancers,

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:11.670
that sort of thing, which
implies public IP addresses.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.320
But I think there's
a couple of other,

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.450
I don't know, just
words, like terminology,

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.353
that we should talk about a bit.

00:09:17.353 --> 00:09:18.520
CARTER MORGAN: What you got?

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.330
BRIAN DORSEY: And one of
those is VPC networks.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:22.980
And then the other
concept is how

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.710
you connect to these
networks from other places.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:26.460
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:30.390
I hear virtual private
cloud all the time.

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:35.020
I'm confused, because I thought
that's what a Google Cloud

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:35.520
network--

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:35.760
BRIAN DORSEY: Cloud is?

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:36.593
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:09:36.593 --> 00:09:39.910
What is Virtual Private Cloud?

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:41.830
BRIAN DORSEY: I think
in this context,

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.980
it's easiest just to think of
it as the collection of services

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:46.810
that provide networking for us.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:51.280
So a VPC network is a security
boundary, if you will.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.490
It's the name of
our networking bits.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:56.290
CARTER MORGAN: So everything.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:57.790
BRIAN DORSEY: So
kind of everything.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.500
But to be more concrete, since
that's kind of vague, when

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:06.160
you create a project and you
create a VM, by default, there

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:10.390
is a network that is created.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:14.110
And so we just call that
the default network.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:18.950
And it provides private,
nonroutable IP addresses.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:23.590
So the public internet is all on
the main pool of IP addresses.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.740
And there's a couple pools that
are never meant to be routed.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:29.900
So routers will never send them
across the public internet.

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:33.760
So we use one of those, the
10-dot series of networks,

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:37.630
as a default and
expose that to you.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.540
And all of your VMs, by
default, live on that network,

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:41.420
wherever they are.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.600
So whatever zone
they're in, they're

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.400
all on the same 10-dot network.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.260
CARTER MORGAN: Oh wow.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.290
So I can just address them
like they're right next to me

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:50.900
everywhere?

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.010
What about-- OK, here's
an interesting one.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:59.080
So if I have a VPC, and
you have a VPC, 10.1.1--

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:00.490
is that shared?

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:03.018
Or do we get our
own separate one?

00:11:03.018 --> 00:11:03.810
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:07.030
So that is a nonroutable
private address space,

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.660
so you can't ever have
collisions with other people.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:13.500
And my 10.1.1.1 will be
different than yours.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.240
And the things will never
move across that way.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.060
CARTER MORGAN: That simplifies
a lot of networking.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:21.960
BRIAN DORSEY: Yes.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:22.710
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.790
I want to be
skeptical but that--

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:26.290
OK.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:27.460
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, so let
me add one onto that.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:28.680
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:30.555
BRIAN DORSEY: Another
thing along those lines

00:11:30.555 --> 00:11:33.240
is that when you create a
VM, you give it a hostname.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:37.620
And we automatically
provide DNS of name lookup

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:39.910
based on those names
for all the machines.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:43.050
So you can just ping
another VM by its hostname

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.630
wherever it is in the
world, in whatever network,

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.830
and it just simplifies a
lot of things in automation.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:49.830
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, OK.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:53.310
What about if I wanted to bring
on my own IP address, so public

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:53.850
IP address?

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.017
Because this is all private.

00:11:55.017 --> 00:11:56.100
Is there a way to do that?

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:56.880
BRIAN DORSEY: Yes.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.020
Yep.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:11:58.680
There sure is.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:03.607
And so we have both this concept
of ephemeral IP addresses.

00:12:03.607 --> 00:12:05.190
So if you just make
a machine and say,

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:06.648
I want to have a
public IP address,

00:12:06.648 --> 00:12:09.120
and I don't care what
it is, you can do that.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.370
And then most of the
time, you actually want

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:13.710
a kind of long-lived
address so that maybe you

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:17.370
can give it a DNS entry
and make it mywebsite.com,

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.100
instead of a series of numbers.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:24.658
And those can be either
attached to a particular VM when

00:12:24.658 --> 00:12:25.200
you startup--

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:28.020
and if you just have one
computer, that's handy.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.780
But often, you actually attach
it to the load balancer.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.900
And the load balancer becomes
that long-lived, stable

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.085
endpoint, and the machines
come and go behind the scenes.

00:12:37.085 --> 00:12:38.460
CARTER MORGAN:
OK, that's smooth.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:40.770
So how do I get
started with this?

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.990
How do I take--

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.270
say I have something,
an on-prem system.

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.400
I want to connect to
Google Cloud's network.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:48.675
How do I do this?

00:12:48.675 --> 00:12:49.710
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:52.180
So I'll take that in two phases.

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:55.500
So getting started, the
networking is there by default.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.740
And then you add the pieces as
you need them-- rules, or load

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.370
balancers, or what have you.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:04.380
But this connectivity
bit, that's interesting.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:07.110
So the main one I
talked about so far

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.090
is via the public internet.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:11.340
And we try to get off the
internet as quick as we can,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.920
but things are
routed across there.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:17.310
And that's using just the
normal wide-open protocol.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:19.872
So you may want to
use something that's

00:13:19.872 --> 00:13:22.080
encrypted these days, that
sort of thing, if you can.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.683
But it's running across
the public internet.

00:13:24.683 --> 00:13:27.100
Usually, that's not what people
want when you think about,

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.760
hey, I want to connect
my servers to the Cloud.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:34.040
And so we have a couple of
different ways of doing that.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:38.080
One is setting up a VPN,
a Virtual Private Network.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:43.790
And we have a hosted
IPsec VPN termination.

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:46.480
So you can use the
IPsec protocol, using

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.160
whatever hardware or
software you have,

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.740
and connect that to
the Cloud network.

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.353
And then your
machine's on one end

00:13:52.353 --> 00:13:54.520
and your Cloud machines can
just talk to each other,

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:55.937
as if they're on
the same network.

00:13:55.937 --> 00:13:59.840
CARTER MORGAN: And then you said
that was two of the main ones.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:01.355
There's another?

00:14:01.355 --> 00:14:02.980
BRIAN DORSEY: And
then there's another.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:05.710
So that's still running
across the public internet,

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:07.360
but encrypted.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:10.660
So it's safe, but
occasionally, you

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:13.630
have a use case where you
need to get the latency down

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:15.550
as low as you possibly can.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:19.172
And there's nothing that
really beats co-location.

00:14:19.172 --> 00:14:21.130
You put the machines in
the same places, right?

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:25.660
So we have something called
Cloud Interconnect that allows

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:31.638
you to get rackspace in--

00:14:31.638 --> 00:14:33.055
CARTER MORGAN:
Inside a warehouse?

00:14:33.055 --> 00:14:34.715
BRIAN DORSEY: --a
hosting provider.

00:14:34.715 --> 00:14:36.340
Well, inside a hosting
provider that we

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:40.030
have a connection with or
with partners who do the same.

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:42.400
And so you get
right there, where

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.030
the network's terminated, and
never leave their private data

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:46.720
centers.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:47.320
CARTER MORGAN: All right, Brian.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:48.400
I got to be honest.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:51.700
It's getting hard to be
skeptical at this point.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.610
You're doing a good job.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:55.480
It sounds cool.

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:58.263
I guess I have-- if I had
to ask one last question,

00:14:58.263 --> 00:14:59.680
it's going to sound
like a gotcha,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.720
but I don't mean it to be.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.360
What are the advantages
or why would someone

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:07.060
want to work at this level,
the infrastructure level,

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:08.900
over working at, say,
the Kubernetes level?

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:11.150
Where I could still work
with the Cloud load balancer,

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:12.650
but a lot of choices
have been made.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.620
I don't have to deal with
all the nitty-gritty.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:15.412
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:15:15.412 --> 00:15:17.050
And there's some
real big advantages

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.720
to having a curated experience.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.340
Kubernetes sets a
bunch of stuff up.

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:25.690
It handles authentication
even between the connections

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:29.620
and gives you these
nice abstractions.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:31.700
And so you get this
endpoint you can connect to.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:33.370
So if you're already
running a cluster,

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:36.025
I think you should
keep doing that.

00:15:36.025 --> 00:15:39.880
That said, there's
a lot of apps that

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.730
just don't run in Kubernetes,
maybe yet or maybe ever.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:46.610
And sometimes, you want to use
a lot of these same patterns.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:51.460
And I think this static endpoint
of a load balancer in front of,

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:53.680
even if it's just one
machine, is actually

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.790
a very useful pattern.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:16:00.495
And so I think it's worth
keeping that in mind.

00:16:00.495 --> 00:16:01.870
There's a few
additional options.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.080
I don't think those
are super key.

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:07.990
But I think this compatibility
with other things that

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.493
are going on-- if you've
got a bunch of services

00:16:10.493 --> 00:16:12.910
in Kubernetes, and you want
to add a few more that are not

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:16.580
in Kubernetes, it's really
useful to be able to do that.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:19.450
And then I think what we
talked about earlier, where

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.480
sometimes you have a system
that just doesn't have

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.460
as many moving parts and
you don't necessarily

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.790
want to setup a whole cluster to
do that, it's a really nice way

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:32.422
to get similar patterns
with way less complexity.

00:16:32.422 --> 00:16:34.130
CARTER MORGAN: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:36.490
And I like what you
said about Kubernetes

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:40.720
as a curated approach to Cloud.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:42.670
You don't always want
or need that curation.

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:44.710
And so it sounds like
working at this level,

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:46.930
you get a lot of great power--

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:47.590
great power.

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:48.220
You get power.

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:49.990
You get flexibility.

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.620
And that comes with
responsibility.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:54.010
So if you're a strength
trainer or a Yogi,

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:56.888
know that you have
responsibilities.

00:16:56.888 --> 00:16:58.430
BRIAN DORSEY:
Actually, exactly that.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.305
And I want to just riff
on that for a moment.

00:17:00.305 --> 00:17:03.040
I think that concept
you described

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:05.890
there is true across the VMs.

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:08.496
So you get a little
more control and you

00:17:08.496 --> 00:17:10.329
have to do a little
more, or you can kind of

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:12.875
work up the layers, if
that's the right fit for you.

00:17:12.875 --> 00:17:13.750
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:15.542
This is why I like
talking with you, Brian.

00:17:15.542 --> 00:17:18.218
We riff, and it
makes me very happy.

00:17:18.218 --> 00:17:19.760
Thinking of these
riffs in the future

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:22.327
and why I think we
should do this again,

00:17:22.327 --> 00:17:24.619
you said you want to talk
about security in the future.

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:25.819
So let's definitely do that.

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:27.277
What else do we
need to talk about?

00:17:27.277 --> 00:17:28.450
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:29.710
I think the next thing
I want to talk about,

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:31.960
just because I'm so excited
about them, is the disks.

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:34.630
I want to go into more
detail about how they work

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:37.060
and what extra
capabilities they give you

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:38.545
that you might not
be thinking of.

00:17:38.545 --> 00:17:40.270
CARTER MORGAN: You
really love disks.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:41.500
BRIAN DORSEY: I do.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:41.820
CARTER MORGAN: You know what?

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:42.320
All right.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.690
Next episode, why don't
we talk about disks?

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:45.920
BRIAN DORSEY: Let's do it.

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:46.350
CARTER MORGAN: All right.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.380
Well, y'all, thank you all for
listening and checking this

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:48.880
out.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.350
Brian, you're slowly but
surely convincing me.

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:53.930
I'm not happy to admit
it, but here we are.

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:55.970
Thank you again for
coming in and talking.

00:17:55.970 --> 00:17:56.600
BRIAN DORSEY: Thank you much.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:57.800
See you all next episode.

